Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/physics_preliminary_examinations/response/1162943/attach/3/Twain.7z?cookie_passthrough=1

The cover identifies the ordinary sitting on 5 June 2013. All questions and all Section B alternatives are covered, including the nested Roman subparts.

== 1
{scope}

=== Solution

Rationalizing the denominator gives
$$
\frac1{1+i\sqrt3}=\frac{1-i\sqrt3}{4}.
$$
Hence its <real part>, <imaginary part>, <complex modulus>, and <principal argument> are respectively
$$
\boxed{\frac14,\quad-\frac{\sqrt3}{4},\quad\frac12,\quad-\frac\pi3.}
$$
The quadrant matters: the <real part> is positive and the <imaginary part> negative. For the exponential expression, <Euler's formula> gives $e^{2+i\pi/4}=e^2(1+i)/\sqrt2$, so the same four quantities are
$$
\boxed{\frac{e^2}{\sqrt2},\quad\frac{e^2}{\sqrt2},\quad e^2,\quad\frac\pi4.}
$$
General <complex arguments> are obtained from each stated <principal argument> by adding $2\pi k$, $k\in\mathbb Z$.

== 2
{scope}

=== Solution

Let $w$ satisfy $\sin w=z$ and put $t=e^{iw}\ne0$. The exponential definition of the <complex sine> gives
$$
\frac{t-t^{-1}}{2i}=z,\qquad t^2-2izt-1=0.
$$
The <quadratic formula> therefore gives $t=iz\pm\sqrt{1-z^2}$. Taking the <complex logarithm> yields the multivalued <complex inverse sine> formula
$$
\boxed{w=-i\log\left(iz\pm\sqrt{1-z^2}\right).}
$$
The two quadratic roots have product $-1$, so neither is zero and their logarithms exist as multivalued quantities. If $w_0$ is one local inverse value, the complete set of values is $w_0+2\pi k$ and $\pi-w_0+2\pi k$, with $k\in\mathbb Z$.

**Branch qualification:** the source's $\pm$ formula describes the <branches of the complex inverse sine>, not two interchangeable expressions for a single chosen inverse branch. At $z=0$, the plus sign with the usual local choices gives $0$, whereas the minus sign with $\log(-1)=i\pi$ gives $\pi$. Thus both signs cannot simultaneously equal the principal inverse at zero. A principal <complex inverse sine> requires compatible square-root and logarithm branches, normalized to $w_0=0$ at zero and continued on the chosen cut domain.

== 3
{scope}

=== Solution

Write all <vectors> in Cartesian components. The first component of the nested <cross product> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)]_x
&=a_y(b_xc_y-b_yc_x)-a_z(b_zc_x-b_xc_z)\\
&=b_x(a_yc_y+a_zc_z)-c_x(a_yb_y+a_zb_z)\\
&=b_x(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-c_x(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b).
\end{aligned}
$$
The missing $a_x$ terms cancel as $-b_xa_xc_x+c_xa_xb_x=0$. Computing the $y$ and $z$ components by cyclic permutation gives the same form, proving the <vector triple product identity>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)
=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)\mathbf b-(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)\mathbf c.}
$$
Apply this expression to the three cyclic terms. Their sum is
$$
(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)\mathbf b-(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)\mathbf c
+(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf a)\mathbf c-(\mathbf b\cdot\mathbf c)\mathbf a
+(\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf b)\mathbf a-(\mathbf c\cdot\mathbf a)\mathbf b=0.
$$
Each coefficient cancels because the <dot product> is symmetric. This is the <Jacobi identity> for the <cross product>.

== 4
{scope}

=== Solution

Let $A\mathbf v=\lambda\mathbf v$ with $\mathbf v\ne0$ and $A^\dagger=-A$. Taking the conjugate of the scalar $\mathbf v^\dagger A\mathbf v$ gives
$$
\lambda^*\mathbf v^\dagger\mathbf v
=\mathbf v^\dagger A^\dagger\mathbf v
=-\lambda\mathbf v^\dagger\mathbf v.
$$
Since $\mathbf v^\dagger\mathbf v>0$, it follows that
$$
\boxed{\lambda^*=-\lambda,\qquad \operatorname{Re}\lambda=0.}
$$
Thus every nonzero <eigenvalue> of an <anti-Hermitian matrix> is purely imaginary; zero is allowed as well.

For $A\mathbf u=\lambda\mathbf u$ and $A\mathbf v=\mu\mathbf v$, compute the same mixed scalar in two ways:
$$
\lambda^*\mathbf u^\dagger\mathbf v
=(A\mathbf u)^\dagger\mathbf v
=\mathbf u^\dagger A^\dagger\mathbf v
=-\mu\mathbf u^\dagger\mathbf v.
$$
Using $\lambda^*=-\lambda$ gives $(\mu-\lambda)\mathbf u^\dagger\mathbf v=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda\ne\mu\ \Longrightarrow\ \mathbf u^\dagger\mathbf v=0.}
$$
The relevant orthogonality uses the complex <inner product>, not the unconjugated bilinear dot product. Repeated <eigenvalues> do not force every possible pair of eigenvectors within that one eigenspace to be orthogonal.

== 5
{scope}

=== Solution

The original <basis> matrix has columns $\mathbf e_1,\mathbf e_2,\mathbf e_3$ and <determinant> $D=3\cdot3-4\cdot4=-7$. Evaluating the defining <cross products> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf e_1'=\left(-\frac37,\frac47,0\right),\quad
\mathbf e_2'=\left(\frac47,-\frac37,0\right),\quad
\mathbf e_3'=(0,0,1).}
$$
For instance, $\mathbf e_2\times\mathbf e_3=(3,-4,0)$, which must be divided by the negative denominator $-7$. These vectors satisfy $\mathbf e_i'\cdot\mathbf e_j=\delta_{ij}$, the defining <reciprocal basis> relations.

Dotting the desired expansion with the reciprocal vectors yields
$$
\boxed{a_1=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf e_1'=1,\qquad
 a_2=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf e_2'=0,\qquad
 a_3=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf e_3'=2.}
$$
Indeed $\mathbf a=\mathbf e_1+2\mathbf e_3$. The original <basis> is not orthonormal, so taking dot products with the original vectors would not give these expansion coefficients.

== 6
{scope}

=== Solution

Here the dependent variable is $x$ and the independent variable is $y$. Multiplying the equation by $2x$ and setting $q=x^2$ gives a linear equation:
$$
\frac{dq}{dy}-\frac4yq=2y.
$$
An <integrating factor> is $y^{-4}$, so
$$
\frac d{dy}(q y^{-4})=2y^{-3},\qquad
q y^{-4}=-y^{-2}+C.
$$
Thus $x^2=Cy^4-y^2$. The initial point $(x,y)=(1,1)$ fixes $C=2$, and the positive branch through that point is
$$
\boxed{x(y)=y\sqrt{2y^2-1},\qquad y>\frac1{\sqrt2}.}
$$
Equivalently, inverting this branch gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac12\sqrt{1+\sqrt{1+8x^2}},\qquad x>0.}
$$
The source equation contains $xy$ in the denominator. The endpoint with $x=0$ is therefore excluded even though the inverse expression has a finite limit there. A negative-$x$ branch cannot be joined through that endpoint to produce a solution $x(y)$ of the original equation. The interval shown is the maximal real positive branch containing the initial point.

== 7
{scope}

=== Solution

The <characteristic polynomial> of the homogeneous equation factors as $(r-1)(r-3)$, giving the <complementary function> $C_1e^x+C_2e^{3x}$. For a particular solution take $A\cos x+B\sin x$. Comparing cosine and sine coefficients gives $2A-4B=10$ and $4A+2B=0$, hence $A=1,B=-2$. Therefore
$$
y=C_1e^x+C_2e^{3x}+\cos x-2\sin x.
$$
The initial conditions imply $C_1+C_2=2$ and $C_1+3C_2=2$, so $C_1=2,C_2=0$. The required solution is
$$
\boxed{y=2e^x+\cos x-2\sin x.}
$$
It has $y(0)=3$ and $y'(0)=0$, and direct <differentiation> verifies $y''-4y'+3y=10\cos x$.

== 8
{scope}

=== Solution

The second coordinate is already an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $-2$. On the first and third coordinates the <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}2-\lambda&2\\2&-1-\lambda\end{pmatrix}
=\lambda^2-\lambda-6=(\lambda-3)(\lambda+2).
$$
Thus the full <eigenvalues> are $3,-2,-2$. A corresponding <orthonormal eigenbasis> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda=3 & \mathbf v_3=(2,0,1)/\sqrt5\\
\lambda=-2 & \mathbf v_{-2}=(1,0,-2)/\sqrt5,\quad\mathbf e_2=(0,1,0).
\end{array}}
$$
Their pairwise dot products vanish and each has norm one. Substitution checks $A\mathbf v_3=3\mathbf v_3$ and $A\mathbf v_{-2}=-2\mathbf v_{-2}$. The two-dimensional repeated <eigenspace> permits any orthonormal pair spanning the same plane; the displayed choice is convenient because it separates the second coordinate.

== 9
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

For an ordinary real <quadratic form>, split the matrix into its <symmetric part> and <skew-symmetric part>:
$$
A=M+K,\qquad M=\frac{A+A^T}{2},\quad K=\frac{A-A^T}{2}.
$$
The scalar $\mathbf x^TK\mathbf x$ equals its transpose $\mathbf x^TK^T\mathbf x=-\mathbf x^TK\mathbf x$, so it is zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbf x^TA\mathbf x=\mathbf x^TM\mathbf x,\qquad
M=\frac{A+A^T}{2}=M^T.}
$$
This uses transpose because the source form is bilinear. A complex Hermitian form $\mathbf x^\dagger A\mathbf x$ would instead require the conjugate-transpose decomposition.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

The <symmetric part> of the displayed matrix is
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}5&0&2\\0&1&0\\2&0&2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
It equals the matrix of <8> plus $3I$, so the same <orthonormal eigenbasis> gives <eigenvalues> $6,1,1$. In coordinates $u,v,w$ along $\mathbf v_3,\mathbf v_{-2},\mathbf e_2$, respectively, the level set is
$$
6u^2+v^2+w^2=6,\qquad
\boxed{\frac{u^2}{1^2}+\frac{v^2}{(\sqrt6)^2}+\frac{w^2}{(\sqrt6)^2}=1.}
$$
All three <eigenvalues> are positive, so this is an <ellipsoid> with semi-axis lengths $1,\sqrt6,\sqrt6$. The short axis is along $(2,0,1)$, and the plane of the longest axes is $2x_1+x_3=0$.

A natural longest-axis direction in the $x_1x_3$ plane is $\mathbf v_{-2}=(1,0,-2)/\sqrt5$. The angle $\eta$ of a unit vector to the plane $x_1=0$ has $\sin\eta=|v_1|$, giving
$$
\boxed{\eta=\arcsin\frac1{\sqrt5}=\arctan\frac12\simeq26.565^\circ}
$$
for that choice. **Degeneracy qualification:** the two longest semi-axes are equal, so “the longest axis” does not specify a unique direction. The equally valid longest axis $\mathbf e_2$ lies in $x_1=0$ and has angle zero. Generally $\mathbf v=\cos\psi\,\mathbf v_{-2}+\sin\psi\,\mathbf e_2$ is a longest-axis unit vector, with
$$
\boxed{\eta(\psi)=\arcsin\left(\frac{|\cos\psi|}{\sqrt5}\right),\qquad
0\le\eta\le\arctan\frac12.}
$$
This is the <principal-axis freedom in a repeated ellipsoid eigenspace>; a unique answer needs an extra axis-selection convention.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

The coefficient matrix and right-hand side are
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}2&4&3\\2&-2&-3\\-4&-2&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\mathbf b=\begin{pmatrix}4\\0\\-6\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For an <LU decomposition> with unit diagonal in the lower factor, subtract row 1 from row 2, add twice row 1 to row 3, and then add the new row 2 to row 3. The elimination multipliers are $l_{21}=1$, $l_{31}=-2$, and $l_{32}=-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{L=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\1&1&0\\-2&-1&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
U=\begin{pmatrix}2&4&3\\0&-6&-6\\0&0&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplication verifies $LU=A$. Forward substitution in $L\mathbf y=\mathbf b$ gives $y_1=4$, $y_2=-4$, and $y_3=-2$. Back substitution then gives $x_3=-1$, $x_2=5/3$, and $x_1=1/6$:
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y=(4,-4,-2)^T,\qquad
\mathbf x=(1/6,5/3,-1)^T.}
$$
Every pivot is nonzero, so no row permutation is needed and the <linear system> has a unique solution.

== 10
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

<De Moivre's theorem> states that, for real $\theta$ and integer $n$,
$$
\boxed{(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^n=\cos(n\theta)+i\sin(n\theta).}
$$
It follows from $\cos\theta+i\sin\theta=e^{i\theta}$ and exponent multiplication. Negative integers are allowed because this unit-modulus complex number is nonzero. For a general polar number $re^{i\theta}$, its integer power also has the factor $r^n$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Take the <imaginary part> of the eighth-power binomial expansion in <De Moivre's theorem>. With $s=\sin\theta$ and $c=\cos\theta$,
$$
\sin8\theta=8c^7s-56c^5s^3+56c^3s^5-8cs^7
=sc\left[8c^6-56c^4s^2+56c^2s^4-8s^6\right].
$$
Substitute $c^2=1-s^2$ and collect powers:
$$
\sin8\theta=sc\left[8-80s^2+192s^4-128s^6\right].
$$
Therefore the requested leading coefficient is
$$
\boxed{a=-128.}
$$
The other coefficients, if retained as an algebraic check, are $b=192,c=-80,d=8$. No division by $s$ or $c$ is needed, so the identity remains valid when either vanishes.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Set $q=e^{i\theta}/2$. The required sine sum is the <imaginary part> of a finite <geometric series>:
$$
\sum_{n=1}^Nq^n=\frac{q(1-q^N)}{1-q}
=\frac{(q-q^{N+1})(1-\overline q)}{|1-q|^2}.
$$
The denominator is $(5-4\cos\theta)/4$. The numerator's <imaginary part> is
$$
\frac12\sin\theta-\frac{\sin((N+1)\theta)}{2^{N+1}}
+\frac{\sin(N\theta)}{2^{N+2}}.
$$
Dividing and multiplying numerator and denominator by $2^N$ proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^N\frac{\sin(n\theta)}{2^n}
=\frac{2^{N+1}\sin\theta+\sin(N\theta)-2\sin((N+1)\theta)}{2^N(5-4\cos\theta)}.}
$$
For real $\theta$, $5-4\cos\theta\ge1$, so there is no exceptional angle requiring a limiting interpretation. At $N=1$ the expression reduces to $\sin\theta/2$.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

For $z=x+iy\ne0$, $z^{-1}=(x-iy)/(x^2+y^2)$. The first constraint is $x=x^2+y^2$ and the second is $-y=x^2+y^2$. Completing the squares gives
$$
\boxed{\left(x-\frac12\right)^2+y^2=\frac14,\qquad
x^2+\left(y+\frac12\right)^2=\frac14,\qquad z\ne0.}
$$
These <circles> have centres $(1/2,0)$ and $(0,-1/2)$ and radius $1/2$. Their algebraic intersections satisfy $x=-y$ and $2x^2=x$, giving zero and $(1-i)/2$. Zero is excluded from both original <loci>, so their only actual intersection is
$$
\boxed{z=\frac{1-i}{2}.}
$$
At $(x,y)=(1/2,-1/2)$ the two circle normals are $(0,-1)$ and $(1,0)$, so the tangent directions are perpendicular. The intersection angle is $\boxed{\pi/2}$. This is also an instance of the <orthogonality of reciprocal-coordinate circles> under complex inversion.

The first small circle touches the unit <circle> at $z=1$, and the second at $z=-i$. On the first, $r^2=x\le r$; on the second, $r^2=-y\le r$. Thus each lies entirely within $r\le1$, attaining equality only at its stated point. They are internally tangent to the unit <circle> and do not cross it. The original sketch marks the excluded common origin with an open symbol and the genuine intersection with a filled symbol.

\Image[2013-reciprocal-coordinate-circles.png]
{title=Original <Argand diagram> of reciprocal-coordinate <circles>, their orthogonal intersection, and tangency to the unit <circle>}

== 11
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Each <Pauli matrix> has zero diagonal sum and <determinant> $-1$. Direct multiplication gives $\sigma_x^2=\sigma_y^2=\sigma_z^2=I$. Thus all requested quantities, for each $j=x,y,z$, are
$$
\boxed{\det\sigma_j=-1,\qquad \operatorname{tr}\sigma_j=0,\qquad
\sigma_j^2=I,\qquad\sigma_j^{-1}=\sigma_j.}
$$
For example, $\sigma_y^2=\operatorname{diag}((-i)i,i(-i))=I$, checking that its imaginary entries do not introduce a minus sign in the square.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

The cyclic products obtained by direct <matrix multiplication> are $\sigma_x\sigma_y=i\sigma_z$, $\sigma_y\sigma_z=i\sigma_x$, and $\sigma_z\sigma_x=i\sigma_y$. Reversing any two distinct indices changes the sign; equal-index products are $I$. Therefore every pair is covered by
$$
\boxed{\{\sigma_i,\sigma_j\}=2\delta_{ij}I,\qquad
[\sigma_i,\sigma_j]=2i\sum_k\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k.}
$$
Here $\{A,B\}$ is the <anticommutator> and $[A,B]$ the <commutator>. Explicitly the three diagonal <anticommutators> are $2I$, all six off-diagonal ones are zero, and all three diagonal <commutators> vanish. The cyclic <commutators> are $2i\sigma_z,2i\sigma_x,2i\sigma_y$, respectively, with their reversed partners negative. The convention is $\epsilon_{xyz}=1$.

=== c
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Combine the diagonal and cyclic products from <11/b> into $\sigma_i\sigma_j=\delta_{ij}I+i\sum_k\epsilon_{ijk}\sigma_k$. Distribute the coefficients in the two Pauli-vector factors:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sigma(\mathbf a)\sigma(\mathbf b)
&=\sum_{i,j}a_ib_j\sigma_i\sigma_j\\
&=\left(\sum_i a_ib_i\right)I
+i\sum_k\left(\sum_{i,j}\epsilon_{ijk}a_ib_j\right)\sigma_k.
\end{aligned}
$$
The inner sums are precisely the components of $\mathbf a\times\mathbf b$, so
$$
\boxed{\sigma(\mathbf a)\sigma(\mathbf b)
=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)I+i\sigma(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b).}
$$
Interchanging the two factors shows that their <commutator> is $2i\sigma(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)$. The three <Pauli matrices> are linearly independent: the entries of $\sigma(\mathbf c)$ are $c_z,c_x-ic_y,c_x+ic_y,-c_z$, which vanish together only when $\mathbf c=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{[\sigma(\mathbf a),\sigma(\mathbf b)]=0
\iff\mathbf a\times\mathbf b=0
\iff\mathbf a,\mathbf b\text{ are linearly dependent}.}
$$
For nonzero real <vectors>, this means parallel or antiparallel directions. The dot and cross products here are bilinear; no complex conjugates are inserted if complex coefficients are considered.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Setting $\mathbf b=\mathbf a$ in <11/c/i> removes the <cross product> term and gives $\sigma(\mathbf a)^2=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)I$. Repeated multiplication therefore yields, for nonnegative integers $m$,
$$
\boxed{\sigma(\mathbf a)^{2m}=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)^mI,\qquad
\sigma(\mathbf a)^{2m+1}=(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)^m\sigma(\mathbf a).}
$$
The zeroth power is $I$. If negative powers are intended, they require $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a\ne0$, and the same parity formulas then follow from $\sigma(\mathbf a)^{-1}=\sigma(\mathbf a)/(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf a)$. Every nonzero real vector meets that condition; a nonzero complex null vector need not. For example, $\mathbf a=(1,i,0)$ has $\sigma(\mathbf a)^2=0$ and no inverse.

For a real <unit vector> $\hat{\mathbf n}$, let $S=\sigma(\hat{\mathbf n})$. Then $S^2=I$, so even powers are $I$ and odd powers are $S$. Split the absolutely convergent <matrix exponential> into its even and odd series:
$$
\begin{aligned}
e^{i\theta S}
&=I\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^m\theta^{2m}}{(2m)!}
+iS\sum_{m=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^m\theta^{2m+1}}{(2m+1)!}\\
&=\boxed{\cos\theta\,I+i\sin\theta\,\sigma(\hat{\mathbf n})}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The result holds for every real $\theta$, not merely small angles. Its two scalar series are the ordinary cosine and sine series.

== 12
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The second equation gives $y=t+x'-x$. Differentiating it gives $y'=1+x''-x'$. Substitution into the first equation cancels the $x'$ terms and the constant forcing, leaving $x''+x=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{x=A\cos t+B\sin t,\qquad
y=t-A(\sin t+\cos t)+B(\cos t-\sin t).}
$$
This has two independent constants, as expected for the first-order <linear system of differential equations>. At zero, the conditions give $A=0$ and $B=1$. Thus the required particular solution is
$$
\boxed{x=\sin t,\qquad y=t+\cos t-\sin t.}
$$
Checking directly, $x'+y'+x=\cos t+(1-\sin t-\cos t)+\sin t=1$, while $-x'+x+y=t$. This also verifies the signs introduced during elimination.

=== b
{scope}

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

On an interval with $x\ne-1$, an <integrating factor> is $(x+1)^{-2}$. Multiplication gives
$$
\frac d{dx}\left(\frac{y}{(x+1)^2}\right)=x+1.
$$
Integration therefore yields
$$
\boxed{y=\frac12(x+1)^4+C(x+1)^2.}
$$
The polynomial formula has a smooth continuation at $x=-1$, but the original coefficient $2/(x+1)$ is undefined there. Solution intervals for the stated equation must avoid that point, and the constants on its two sides need not agree.

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

The logarithm requires $x>0$ for real-valued solutions. Put $v=xy$. Since $v'=xy'+y$, the equation becomes $v'=v^2\log x$. For a nonzero branch, separation gives
$$
-\frac1v=\int\log x\,dx=x\log x-x+C_0.
$$
Renaming the constant and restoring $y=v/x$ gives
$$
\boxed{y=\frac1{x(C+x-x\log x)}}.
$$
Dividing by $v^2$ would omit the equilibrium $v=0$, so the full solution set also includes $\boxed{y\equiv0}$. The nonzero branches are defined on connected intervals with $x>0$ and $C+x-x\log x\ne0$. A regular solution cannot switch from a nonzero branch to zero at an ordinary point, because the right-hand side is locally Lipschitz in $v$ there. These domain restrictions are part of the full real solution.
